WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.810
If you haven't finished watching Stranger
Things 2, turn back now!

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.010
You can definitely expect spoilers ahead.

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:10.940
The second season of Stranger Things wrapped
about as neatly as you could hope for; Hawkins,

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.590
Indiana is once again safe from the threat
of the Upside-Down, and Mike and Eleven finally

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:16.699
got that dance.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.140
Yeah, we're still hurting inside for Bob…

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.500
"And...splash."

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.800
The rest of the cast made it through the season
with nothing worse than a broken nose and

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.249
a
few bruised egos.

00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:32.580
But those last few episodes came and went
like a lightning strike, so let's take a moment

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.750
to slow it down and look at some of the finer
points of what happened at the end of Stranger

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.410
Things 2.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.410
Looming shadow

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.230
Let's start by talking about the interdimensional
elephant in the room—the Shadow Monster.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:49.420
The final frames of the last episode essentially
act as a promise for Season 3, reminding us

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.790
that even though the immediate threat from
the shadow monster has been stopped, he's

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.070
still out there in the Upside-Down, looming
over Hawkins.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:00.300
It wasn't quite the cliffhanger we got in
the first season, with a freshly rescued Will

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.890
still tormented by visions and coughing slugs
into the sink, but it proves that the Mind

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.240
Flayer isn't quite finished with the residents
of Hawkins.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.170
As showrunner Ross Duffer explained it:

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.300
"They've shut the door on the Mind Flayer,
but not only is it still there in the Upside

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.969
Down, it's very much aware of the kids, and
particularly Eleven.

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:21.539
It had not encountered her and her powers
until that final episode.

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:23.510
Now, it knows that she's out there."

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:28.110
So that… um… strange thing is contained,
but it's far from defeated.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.280
And if he found a way into our world once,
chances are he'll be able to do it again,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.729
gate or no gate.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:38.490
As we saw several times in Season 1—such
as when the demogorgon broke through the wall

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.789
of the school—the membrane between the worlds
isn't particularly strong where these things

00:01:42.789 --> 00:01:44.500
are concerned.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.500
Closing the gate

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.540
Thanks to Eleven, the gate between Hawkins
and the Upside-Down is definitively closed…

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:50.540
for now.

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:54.000
With the gate closed, all of the Shadow Monster's
influence over our world is apparently cut

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.000
off as well.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.350
We see the demodogs go limp and drop down
the tunnel, and the vines in the tunnel shrivel

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.350
up.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.899
That doesn't necessarily mean the Shadow Monster
can't find a way back in, but it's a good

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.760
start.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:05.760
Power surge

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:10.399
Over the course of two seasons, we've seen
Eleven's power grow steadily, and it's also

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:15.180
apparent that she's much more comfortable
with the humdrum everyday acts of telekinesis—she

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.220
doesn't get a nosebleed anymore with every
little door she slams shut, for example.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.500
But closing the gate clearly pushed Eleven
to her limits—and revealed some of the true

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.370
power of her abilities.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.879
Like that whole floating thing.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:31.420
What's unclear is whether Eleven is channeling
her rage or exorcising it in that moment.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.069
We know that she's focusing on some of the
darkest moments from her past, and earlier

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:39.349
in the season Kali tried to get her to build
her anger as the source of her power.

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:43.040
"I want you to find something from your life.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.260
Something that angers you.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.200
"Now channel it."

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:50.680
Is that what she's doing when she fights back
the Shadow Monster?

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.010
Or is she releasing all that rage once and
for all?

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:55.010
Broken hive mind

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.010
Whatever the Shadow Monster is, it controls
everything around it through a hive mind,

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.400
from the demodogs to the vines spreading out
beneath Hawkins.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.260
It's a pretty scary setup, as we see near
the end of the season.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:10.650
At a moment's notice, the Shadow Monster can
send an army of killer demodogs anywhere in

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:11.650
Hawkins.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.880
But we're also led to believe that it doesn't
have perfect control over its minions, either.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.780
When Dustin runs into his pet demodog, Dart,
in the tunnels, Dart lets the kids pass, showing

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:24.340
that it can break the Shadow Monster's control
over it if it wants to.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.459
That's an important detail that may very well
come into play in the third season.

00:03:28.459 --> 00:03:31.709
Hopefully, the kids will have plenty of candy
bars handy.

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:32.709
Cooking the virus

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:37.680
While Hopper and Eleven head to the Hawkins
Lab to shut the gate, Joyce, Jonathan, and

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.740
Nancy go to Hopper's cabin with a comatose
Will and turn up the heat on the thing living

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.620
inside him—literally.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:48.000
By using all those heaters, Joyce, Jonathan,
and Nancy are trying to sweat the virus out

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.329
of Will like a fever.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:50.329
More or less.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:51.629
"We need to burn it out of him."

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:54.870
It gets pretty brutal, but they're also saving
Will's life.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.670
If that thing was still inside Will when Eleven
shut the gate, there's a good chance Will

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.010
would have died along with the rest of the
Shadow Monster's hive mind.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:06.819
Sure, Hopper was waiting for Jonathan's signal
to prevent exactly that, but would he have

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:08.060
waited forever?

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.400
Tunnel distraction

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.670
Of course, the rest of the kids aren't idle
during the climax.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.980
Instead of waiting at the Byers house as ordered,
they head out to the pumpkin patch to distract

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.120
the Shadow Monster while Eleven and Hopper
work their way toward the gate in Hawkins

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.120
Lab.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.850
Their plan: just set a bunch of stuff on fire
and hope the Shadow Monster sends all the

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:27.050
demodogs their way.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.699
Yeah, not exactly the best plan in the world,
but it works—maybe even better than they

00:04:31.699 --> 00:04:32.699
expected.

00:04:32.699 --> 00:04:36.419
The timing could have been a total coincidence—we'll
grant that—but the moment they set the tunnels

00:04:36.419 --> 00:04:41.590
on fire just happens to be the same moment
the Shadow Monster finally leaves Will.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.370
The truth is out...sort of

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.620
During the epilogue, one month after the events
of the climax, we see a string of government

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:53.080
trucks leaving a now-locked Hawkins Lab, while
a news anchor voiceover explains that the

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:56.050
government admitted to killing Barbara from
Season 1.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:00.120
So Jonathan and Nancy's plan worked--they
got the truth about Hawkins Lab out to the

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.120
public.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.780
And also like they planned, it wasn't the
whole truth.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.240
Not by a long shot.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:09.150
Instead of announcing that Barbara was killed
by a monster that came through an interdimensional

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.800
rift at the lab, the news claims that she
was accidentally poisoned by an "experimental

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.310
chemical asphyxiant."

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:19.841
Because as crazy conspiracy man Murray Bauman
explained in Chapter 5: sometimes you have

00:05:19.841 --> 00:05:22.490
to water down the truth to make people believe
it.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.930
"Just like this drink here.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.030
We make it more tolerable."

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:32.140
The upshot is the same, however: by all appearances,
Hawkins Lab is closed for good.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:36.300
Finally, the citizens of Hawkins get go about
their lives without the government spying

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.860
on everything they do​.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.169
Thanks for watching!

00:05:39.169 --> 00:05:41.940
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.110
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

